WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.110
Earth is a solar powered planet.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:09.680
At the bottom of the food chain, plants use
photosynthesis to convert sunlight into

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.970
chemical energy.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.680
Food for the rest of us.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:19.910
I like eating both grass and wheat, and I
also fancy the odd hamburger from a cow that

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:20.990
has eaten that grass.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.820
This whole ecosystem is powered by sunshine.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:31.730
But recent studies have shed new light on how
life developed under our sun.

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:36.170
Specifically the role of ultraviolet light.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:42.670
Uv radiation is useful for breaking molecules
up and triggering reactions.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.390
Maybe that played a role in the origin of
life.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.270
It breaks down simple organic molecules, and
then they can rebuild themselves into things

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.500
that are more complex.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.740
If you do that over and over again,
eventually you somehow get life.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.750
Scientists think life on Earth started around
4 billion years ago.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.530
A time when Earth's atmosphere gave little
protection.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.050
Uv radiation levels were 100 times higher.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.220
Was essential for the development of life's
code DNA.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:32.170
We know that life on Earth stores information
in DNA and then uses that information to

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.910
build proteins.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.770
So we have the blueprints and the bricks.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.030
The blueprints are the DNA and the bricks
are the proteins.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:47.710
But we think that the first life on Earth, we
used a chemical which is much simpler.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.100
This simpler chemical was RNA.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.340
Dna is single stranded forefather.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.930
Rna is almost like a two for one offer.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:06.580
It does both of the fundamental things you
need for a cell in the same compound.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.400
So it was simultaneously the bricks and the
blueprint.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.130
Unlike other molecules, RNA is more resistant
to the high UV environment of early Earth,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.840
allowing it to flourish.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.620
Rna eventually evolved into DNA, and life
started.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.440
To have life on the planet.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.190
One important consideration is a certain
amount of light that's going to be needed,

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.560
and a certain type of light that's going to
be needed.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:48.220
So if all stars emit some UV radiation, can
life start around any star?

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.680
When we think about looking for places that
are conducive for life, we want to find a

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.140
planet that might have enough UV radiation.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.030
So the star is is bright enough or close
enough that it's providing enough energy to

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:02.260
the surface for life.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.270
But we also don't want to have too much UV
radiation.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.840
It seems you need just the right amounts of
UV.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:19.820
The most common stars in the galaxy are red
dwarf stars.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.620
If red dwarf stars can harbour life on
planets around them.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.420
There's an awful lot of real estate like
that in our galaxy.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.320
Red dwarf stars could be good for life's
chance of conquering the universe in a number

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:38.980
of ways.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.250
One. They represent over three quarters of
all stars in the universe.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.270
Two they live for over a thousand times
longer than sunlike stars.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.130
And three, they seem to have rocky planets
around them much more often than sunlike

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:53.940
stars do.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.530
Those are the pros.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.980
But red dwarf stars also have cons.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.250
For instance, they might not be bright
enough for life to begin.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:10.040
Some of the red dwarf stars that we know emit
less ultraviolet light than the sun.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.040
They don't give off much UV light at all.

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.830
Maybe on a planet around them there isn't
enough energy to get life started.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.340
Red dwarf stars are also much more
temperamental.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:25.220
They can go from being gentle and quiet to
having violent outbursts.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.820
Stellar flares.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.720
These types of stars have incredibly strong
flares.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:39.140
That means they're shooting off a bunch of
energetic particles and radiation and light

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.780
that's baking the surfaces of those planets.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:48.070
If the star is just bombarding the surface
with UV, then it will destroy all of those.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.690
Those things necessary for life.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.600
It will actually destroy the life itself.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:03.030
These stellar flares could strip away a
planet's atmosphere, sterilizing the surface.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:08.680
More research is needed.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:14.350
But for now, the odds of life thriving
around dwarf stars are a toss up.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:24.220
So far, the only thing we know is that there
is one kind of star that's definitely right

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.010
for life our sun.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.800
We know of life in one place in the universe.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:30.880
And that's that's here.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.660
That's Earth.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.490
Only 4% of stars in the universe are like our
sun.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:45.550
So if life can only get started around these
rare medium sized stars, the chances are not

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:54.410
looking good. But life may have an ace up
its sleeve.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:59.540
What if life can start on just one planet
and then spread?

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:06.770
What if life travels across the cosmos
looking for planets to conquer?

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.820
Earth is our only example of life emerging
anywhere in the universe.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.890
But what if life on earth didn't start on
Earth at all?

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.140
There's one idea that life.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.530
On earth actually didn't get going here, but
was delivered from space.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.470
Scientists call this theory panspermia.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:35.110
The idea of panspermia essentially talks
about the transferral of life throughout the

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:36.040
cosmos.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:44.510
We know asteroids and comets carry organic
molecules, but could they carry life itself?

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.140
What if life starts on one planet?

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.430
Can it actually get itself to a nearby
planet?

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.500
Is it possible that meteorites could
actually transport living beings.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.640
For life to travel around the cosmos?

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.700
First, it needs to take flight.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:07.980
An asteroid is on a collision course with an
inhabited planet.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:12.750
So what happens if there's a huge cataclysmic
collision on a planet?

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.360
Material is blasted off into space.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:25.660
The impact might kill life on the surface of
that planet, but it's possible some bacteria

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:30.880
might escape hitching a ride on chunks of
the planet's surface.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.360
A meteorite being ejected from a planet after
an asteroid impact.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.320
I mean, that's not going to be an easy ride,
but it turns out it's not as bad as you

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:40.800
think.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.460
Some bacteria are very, very hard to kill.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.290
Some we even don't know how to kill.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.760
Even the impact that actually threw that
rock into space for bacteria.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:56.370
No problem. If those chunks of rock expelled
during asteroid collisions could actually

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:02.460
hold on to viable organisms, then it really
could change the way in which we think about

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.890
life spreading in the universe.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.510
If the microbes can survive takeoff, then
they can start their journey to a new home.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:16.740
The odds of life conquering the universe
seem to be getting better.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:21.210
The important question now is how long could
that life those bacteria, those

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:25.350
microorganisms inside that rock, survive the
space environment?

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:32.040
Exposure to UV radiation could be fatal,
killing any life on the surface of an

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:38.760
asteroid. But experts think that microbial
passengers could still survive by hiding

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:39.720
underground.

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:45.970
It doesn't take much to shield a
microorganism from UV, just a little bit of

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:51.850
rock and you have enough protection to just
hold on throughout a journey to the next

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.260
body, to your next home.

